<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Widget extension entitlements.

  ONE capability, and the absence of the other one is the point.

  * com.apple.security.application-groups — expanded from HOPPOTTY_APP_GROUP in
    Config/Base.xcconfig. Must be byte-identical to the app's, to the three
    Screen Time extensions', to ScreenTimeIdentifiers.appGroupID and to
    WidgetSnapshotStore.widgetAppGroupID. Scripts/verify-config.sh compares all
    of them, because the failure mode is silent: the container URL comes back
    nil, the widget finds no snapshot, and it draws the empty state forever while
    every target still builds, signs and runs.

  * com.apple.developer.family-controls — DELIBERATELY ABSENT.

    The other three extensions need it because they touch ManagedSettings,
    ManagedSettingsUI or DeviceActivity. This one draws a countdown from a JSON
    file. Adding the entitlement would mean: a fifth App ID in the Family
    Controls distribution request (Docs/Entitlements.md §2), a widget process
    linking frameworks it never calls, and a capability granted to the one
    HopPotty target that is rendered on a locked screen. All cost, no function.

    If a future widget ever needs to know something only ManagedSettings can
    tell it, the answer is for the app to resolve that into the snapshot — the
    same discipline the shield-configuration extension already follows.

  NEVER add: aps-environment (no push, so no ActivityKit push tokens either —
  HopPotty's Live Activities are updated in-process by the app), iCloud
  containers, keychain sharing, or
  com.apple.developer.family-controls.app-and-website-usage.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(HOPPOTTY_APP_GROUP)</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
